# systemd :: services, logs, timers, the user session

## Services
systemctl status <unit> :: state, recent log lines
sudo systemctl start / stop / restart <unit> :: now
sudo systemctl enable --now <unit> :: start at boot and now (disable --now: the opposite)
systemctl --failed :: units that failed
systemctl list-units --type=service :: running services
systemctl cat <unit> :: the unit file (with drop-ins)
sudo systemctl edit <unit> :: add a drop-in override
sudo systemctl daemon-reload :: after editing unit files
sudo systemctl reset-failed :: forget past failures

## Your user's services (Syncthing, the desktop session)
systemctl --user status <unit> :: same commands, with --user, no sudo
systemctl --user list-units :: what runs in your session
systemctl --user show-environment :: the environment desktop apps get

## Logs (journalctl)
journalctl -b :: this boot (-b -1: the previous one)
journalctl -u <unit> -f :: one unit, live
journalctl -p err -b :: errors this boot
journalctl --since "1 hour ago" :: by time ("yesterday", "2026-10-27 21:00")
journalctl --user -b :: your session's apps (Hyprland, waybar…)
journalctl -k :: kernel messages (like dmesg)
journalctl --disk-usage / --vacuum-time=2weeks :: size / shrink it

## Timers
systemctl list-timers :: scheduled jobs and their next run
systemd-run --on-calendar=daily <cmd> :: a one-off scheduled command
systemd-run --user --on-active=30m <cmd> :: in 30 minutes

## Machine
systemctl reboot / poweroff / suspend :: power
systemd-analyze blame :: what slowed the boot
hostnamectl / timedatectl / localectl :: hostname, time, keyboard
loginctl :: sessions and seats
coredumpctl list :: programs that crashed
